<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Western Ganga dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 350–<a href="/years/1000">1000</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Western Gangas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Western Ganga dynasty ruled southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from about 350 to 1000, with capitals at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolar">Kolar</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talakad">Talakad</a>. Often subordinate allies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a> and then of the Rashtrakutas, they were major patrons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>; the colossal statue of Gommateshwara at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shravanabelagola">Shravanabelagola</a> was erected by their minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chavundaraya">Chavundaraya</a>. Their kingdom fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> around 1000.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/western-ganga-dynasty.jpg" alt="Western Ganga dynasty" width="320" height="376" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mlpkr · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Western-ganga-empire-map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The core territory around Vatapi was ruled directly through provinces and districts known as vishaya, but much of the kingdom was held through subordinate dynasties such as the <b>Western Ganga dynasty</b>, the Alupa dynasty and the Sendrakas, who paid tribute and supplied troops.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Dantidurga died c. 756 without a son and was succeeded by his uncle Krishna I, who completed the conquest of the remaining Chalukya lands, subdued the <b>Western Ganga dynasty</b> of southern Karnataka and attacked the Eastern Chalukyas of Vengi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He took advantage of the sack of the Rashtrakuta capital Manyakheta by the Paramara Siyaka II in 972, overthrew the last Rashtrakuta king, Karka II, and then defeated the loyalist resistance led by the <b>Western Ganga</b> ruler Marasimha II, whose candidate Indra IV died in 982.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the south, Jainism flourished in Karnataka and Tamil Nadu in the first millennium CE under dynasties such as the <b>Western Gangas</b>, whose minister Chavundaraya had the great statue of Bahubali carved at Shravanabelagola around 983; Jain monks contributed greatly to early literature in Tamil and Kannada.</blockquote><a href="/religion/jainism">Jainism · Spread</a></li></ul></section>
